{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310805,"nonce":"823M3xYOZC29dE2XFxFVvFhP0I4=","title":"The Q4_K instruction gap vs llama.cpp is the SCALE PATH: ggml keeps scales in vector lanes end-to-end; we round-trip every 6-bit scale ...","message":"Context: the kernel is ISSUE-BOUND (IPC 3.26), so time ∝ dynamic instruction\ncount — see [[perf_q4_kernel_is_spill_bound_not_source_tunable]]. This file is\nthe ranked list of what actually reduces that count.\n\nDefault (8 workers): retired instructions drift **15-22%** across a run\nsequence (914G -\u003e 1113G for the SAME arm). An A/B on this metric produced 5/5\n\"wins\" for a change that was codegen-NEUTRAL on that backend.\n**Use `NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=1`** (`~/perf_solo.sh \u003carm\u003e`): single-worker counts\nrepeat within **0.2-0.6%**. Confirm against objdump counts too.\n\n**I first blamed SpinPool spin-wait. TESTED AND FALSE.** `NUE_POOL_SPINS=1`\n(park almost immediately) made counts WORSE, not better — 918.9/944.7G default\nvs 935.3/1053.6G at spins=1, ttft 11.9/12.0 vs 12.0/13.0 — because early\nparking pays futex wake per dispatch, exactly what the adaptive bound avoids.\nRemaining suspect (UNTESTED): contention retries in `stealLoop` as the box\ngets busier. **Do not repeat the \"it's spin\" claim without measuring.**\n\nChecked after a user challenge. Default profile is `throughput`, so\n`Q4Matmul.poolSpins()` returns 0 = \"platform default\", and\n`SpinPool.spinBudget` resolves via `CpuTopology.poolSpinDefault()` -\u003e\n`rayzor_pool_spin_default` (runtime/src/topology/mod.rs:151), which MEASURES\n`iters_per_us` and `park_wake_us` and multiplies them. On the NUC:\n`iters_per_us=26.5 park_wake_us=191.19 budget=5068 -\u003e clamped=5068` (inside the\n200..50k clamp, so not clamped). `adaptiveTight=true` additionally bounds the\ntight spin by WALL TIME sized from the pool's own EWMA dispatch gap (a FLOOR,\n20..500us). Probe: `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` prints `[pool-spin-calib]`.\nThe hardcoded 20000/2000 in `poolSpins()` apply ONLY to the non-default\n`cooperative`/`latency` profiles.\n\nPer sub-block the emitted scale path is ~7 instructions:\n\n    shr / and / or            6-bit unpack, in a GENERAL-PURPOSE register\n    vmovd  %eax,%xmm1         GPR -\u003e vector\n    vpbroadcastd %xmm1,%xmm6  splat to 4 lanes\n    vpmulld %xmm6,%xmm13,%xmm1\n    vpaddd\n    (+ vmovdqa %xmm0,0x210(%rsp) — broadcast scales SPILL)\n\nx8 sub-blocks. Census of the 3923-insn body agrees: and 162 · shr 140 · or 103\n· vmovd 97 · vpbroadcastd 74 · vpmulld 104.\n\n**ggml never leaves the vector domain**: it extracts all 8 scales into lanes\nwith a couple of pshufb/and ops and multiplies the PACKED sums by the PACKED\nscales once. That is the \"unpack advantage\" — not the nibble extraction (ours\nis already ONE `vgf2p8affineqb`).\n\n\n**1. `SIMD16i8.shuffle(a, idx)` (pshufb) — the keystone, biggest win.**\nWithout it, scales CANNOT be unpacked in vector lanes, which forces the whole\nGPR round-trip above (~40 insns/super-block) AND keeps 8 broadcast vectors\nlive (the spills). LLVM `build_shuffle_vector` already exists in\nllvm_jit_backend.rs (used internally for the sdot lowering, ~line 735) but is\nNOT exposed to Haxe. Exposing it is a std-type + MIR-wrapper job, not new\ncodegen research.\n\n**2. Fused hi/lo weight load — LANDED c6af740b. THE ANSWER IS PER-BACKEND.**\n`subDotVec(wG,false,·)` + `subDotVec(wG,true,·)` each loaded the SAME 32 weight\nbytes. `subDotPair` loads once, extracts both nibbles, returns\n`scLo*dotLo + scHi*dotHi` (integer adds are exact+associative → bit-identical).\n\n    Cranelift  2746G -\u003e 2434G ins (-11.4%) and 2750G -\u003e 2431G (-11.6%), 2/2\n               ttft 98.8s -\u003e 88.6s (-10.3%)\n    LLVM       no dynamic change (single-worker ±0.4%, 1/2);\n               static 3923 -\u003e 3941, vmovdq* 176 -\u003e 192\n\n**LLVM's GVN already CSE'd the duplicate load; Cranelift's baseline tier does\nNOT** (opt_level=\"none\" + single-pass regalloc, no egraph pass —\ncranelift_backend.rs:217-234). I first measured ONLY on LLVM, concluded \"the\nredundancy isn't real\", and reverted it. The user asked \"LLVM fuses, but does\nCranelift?\" — it does not, and the change is worth 11% there.\n**LESSON: 'the optimiser already handles it' is a claim about ONE backend.\nRayzor has four. Verify per-backend before discarding a source-level fix.**\n\n`--preset development` is NOT sufficient: `nue/examples/llama-chat/rayzor.toml`\nsets `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry = true`, and the MANIFEST WINS\nunless `--preset-override-toml` is passed. Three \"Cranelift\" A/Bs were actually\nLLVM (giveaway: instruction counts and ttft matched the LLVM run almost\nexactly). Confirm the tier moved — real Cranelift here is **2.85x the\ninstructions and 3.1x the ttft**. Harness: `~/perf_clif.sh`.\n\n**3. 16-bit lanes (`SIMD8i16` with mul / madd).**\n104 `vpmulld` in the body: 2 uops, ~10-cycle latency on Alder Lake.\nScales are 6-bit (0..63) and sub-block sums fit 16 bits, so `vpmullw`\n(1 uop, ~5 cycles) or better `vpmaddwd` (fuses the multiply and the add)\napplies. Cuts both uop count and the dependency chain.\n\n**4. `SIMD4i32.load/store`.**\n`SIMD16i8` has `load`; `SIMD4i32` does NOT — so every int vector is built via\n`splat`/`make`, i.e. a GPR round-trip. Adding load/store kills the\n`vmovd`+`vpbroadcastd` pairs even before shuffle lands.\n\n**5. Pack/interleave (punpck*) + reduce placement.**\n`SIMD4i32.sum()` emits a vpshufd chain per accumulator. ggml keeps the 8\nsub-block sums packed and reduces ONCE. Needs the same shuffle/unpack family\nas #1.\n\n`SIMD32i8`/`SIMD8i32` exist; the 256-bit dot measured no win — dots 320-\u003e200\nbut total instructions ROSE (+200 movs, register pressure). Consistent with\nthis file: widening the DOT while the SCALE path still round-trips through\nGPRs just adds pressure. Fix the scale path first, then re-try 256-bit.\n\nThe ggml-side description here is from general knowledge of the AVX2 Q4_K\nkernel, NOT from reading their source this session (homebrew ships binaries\nonly). Our side is all measured from disassembly. Confirm the ggml shape\nagainst real source before treating the comparison as fact.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_q4_scales_round_trip_through_gprs` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}